      "script": "Woman: Good morning. I would like some information about your cookery course, please.\nMan: Of course. The course is called Cooking for Beginners, and it runs on Tuesday evenings at the community centre in Mill Road.\nWoman: What time does each class start?\nMan: Classes start at 6.30 and finish at 8.30, so each session lasts two hours.\nWoman: And how long does the whole course last?\nMan: It lasts six weeks. The first class is on the fourteenth of October, and the final class is on the eighteenth of November.\nWoman: How much does it cost?\nMan: The full course costs ninety-five pounds. That price includes all the ingredients, so you do not need to buy any food yourself.\nWoman: That sounds reasonable. Do I need to bring anything with me?\nMan: Just bring an apron and a container, so that you can take your food home afterwards. We provide all the knives, pans and other equipment.\nWoman: I am vegetarian. Will that be a problem?\nMan: Not at all. Every recipe has a vegetarian option, and in the third week the whole class cooks a meat-free curry together.\nWoman: What will we learn in the other weeks?\nMan: In the first week you learn basic knife skills and a simple tomato soup. Later weeks cover fresh pasta, healthy stir-fries and, in the final week, a three-course dinner that students plan themselves.\nWoman: How many students are there in each class?\nMan: A maximum of twelve, so the teacher has time to help everyone individually.\nWoman: Perfect. I would like to book a place, please. My name is Sarah Bennett.\nMan: Thank you, Sarah. I have booked you in. Please arrive fifteen minutes early for the first class so that we can complete the registration form.\nWoman: Who teaches the course?\nMan: The teacher is Mrs Patel, who worked as a chef in local restaurants for fifteen years before she began teaching.\nWoman: That is reassuring. Can I pay in two parts, or must I pay everything today?\nMan: You can pay half today and half before the third class.\nWoman: And is there parking near the centre?\nMan: Yes, there is a small car park behind the building, and the number twelve bus stops directly outside the front door.\nWoman: That sounds very well organised. I think the course will really help me, because at the moment I mostly buy ready meals and I hardly ever cook fresh food.\nLecturer: Good afternoon, everyone. In this short talk I want to explain three simple habits that can improve your diet without costing much money. The first habit is to eat a proper breakfast. Students who eat breakfast concentrate better in morning classes and are less likely to snack on crisps and chocolate later. The second habit is to drink water instead of fizzy drinks. A single can of cola can contain seven teaspoons of sugar, yet water costs almost nothing and contains no calories at all. The third habit is to fill half of your plate with vegetables at lunch and dinner. Vegetables provide fibre and vitamins, and they help you feel full without adding many calories. Finally, try to cook at home at least four times a week. Home-cooked meals usually contain less salt and fat than takeaways, and cooking with family or friends can be enjoyable as well as healthy. Remember, you do not need to change everything at once. Choose one habit, keep it for a month, and then add another.\nLecturer: One more point before you leave. Please read food labels carefully. Many products that look healthy, such as breakfast cereals and fruit yoghurts, in fact contain surprising amounts of hidden sugar and salt. Choose products with short, simple ingredient lists whenever you can. Thank you for listening, and I will see you next week, when we shall discuss the importance of protein for older people.",
      "title": "Joining a Cookery Course and a Talk on Healthy Eating"
